{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1998 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "e9ea72bcef63d5c4311b913192ee64a24ede88b4c0de6fffa1c0c7262006821b" :edb/db-before-t 1997 :edb/db-after-t 1998 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535310 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:46:17.670Z" 13194139535310 true] [17592186171837 1100 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle -3xy^2 , \\: y+\\cos x \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0), (2,2),$ and $(-2,2).$" 13194139535310 true] [17592186171837 1100 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle -3xy^2, \\: y+\\cos x \\right\\rangle$\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0), (2,2)$, and $(-2,2)$." 13194139535310 false] [17592186171837 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nand $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3361/Source/Images/795c7197b03931fd6387f1c32465d057.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -3xy^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) = y+\\cos x\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} =-3y^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): -y \\leq x \\leq y, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-3y^{2} + 1)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{-y}^{y}(-3y^{2} + 1)dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-3y^{2} + 1)[x]_{-y}^{y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-3y^{2} + 1)(y - (-y))dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-3y^{2} + 1) \\cdot 2ydy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-6y^{3} + 2y)dy \\\\\n&= [- 6 \\cdot \\frac{y^{4}}{4} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{3y^{4}}{2} + y^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (-24 + 4) - 0 \\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535310 false] [17592186171837 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/aa822f0b9259d6d9eb5c6f439588e70d4b9bee16b2b868fae36c7cbac72d3c3f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = -3xy^2$ and $Q(x,y) = y+\\cos x .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  -y \\leq x \\leq y, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)[x]_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)(y-(-y))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2y\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-6{y}^{3}+2y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[-6\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{2}+{y}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(-24+4)-0 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535310 true] [17592186171838 1100 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0), (-1,-3), (1,-3)$, and $(1,0)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (7x^2y^6 +x^3)\\, \\mathbf{i} + (-2xy^7+4x^2y) \\, \\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535310 false] [17592186171838 1100 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0), (-1,-3), (1,-3),$ and $(1,0),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (7x^2y^6 +x^3)\\, \\mathbf{i} + (- 2xy^7+4x^2y) \\, \\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535310 true] [17592186171838 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nand $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3361/Source/Images/558dcb377eb7f6945abd065d2bcdb9e0.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 7x^2y^6 +x^3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) =-2xy^7+4x^2y\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = 14xy^{6} + 3x^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= -14xy^{6} + 4x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y): -1\\leq x\\leq 1, \\, -3\\leq y \\leq 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(14xy^{6} + 3x^{2}) + (-14xy^{6} + 4x^{2})dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}∫_{-3}^{0}7x^{2}dydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535310 false] [17592186171838 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/02964b07e45daba2d560fa16d34bcc92ddb8d0e0e83f644f8284dbe9d4b4557b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) =  7x^2y^6 +x^3$ and $Q(x,y) =- 2xy^7+4x^2y.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -1\\leq x\\leq 1, \\, -3\\leq y \\leq 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2})+(-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}7{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535310 true] [17592186171839 1100 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\sin(\\pi y) - x , \\: \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1), (2,-1), (2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" 13194139535310 true] [17592186171839 1100 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\sin(\\pi y) - x, \\: \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy \\right\\rangle$\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1), (2,-1), (2,1)$, and $(0,1)$." 13194139535310 false] [17592186171839 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nand $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3361/Source/Images/16b1ce554d0410d587294f9657e5e669.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\sin(\\pi y) - x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) = \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} =-1, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\, -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-1 + 2x)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}(2x - 1)dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}(x^{2} - x)\\begin{vmatrix}_{0}^{2}dy \\\\ = ∫_{-1}^{1}(2^{2} - 2)dy \\\\ = ∫_{-1}^{1}2dy \\\\ = 2y\\end{vmatrix}_{-1}^{1} \\\\\n&= 2(1) - 2(-1) \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535310 false] [17592186171839 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/2f3374de4a29c62587a79f6286dfe788e64c37e09e9d9b83b3a67b4dac10dc21.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = \\sin(\\pi y) - x$ and $Q(x,y) = \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\, -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({2}^{2}-2)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\,dy \\\\  & =2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1)-2(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535310 true] [17592186172031 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343133 13194139535310 true] [17592186172031 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343140 13194139535310 true] [17592186172031 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343154 13194139535310 true] [17592186172031 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343168 13194139535310 true] [17592186172031 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343175 13194139535310 true] [17592186172032 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343091 13194139535310 true] [17592186172032 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343098 13194139535310 true] [17592186172032 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343105 13194139535310 true] [17592186172032 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343112 13194139535310 true] [17592186172033 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343119 13194139535310 true] [17592186172033 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343126 13194139535310 true] [17592186172033 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343147 13194139535310 true] [17592186172033 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343161 13194139535310 true] [17592186172238 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535310 true] [17592186172238 1100 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" 13194139535310 true] [17592186172238 1100 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$F(x, y) = ⟨e^{y} + 2x^{2}y, e^{x} + 2xy^{2}⟩$\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1, 0), (2, 0), (2, 1)$, and $(-1, 1)$." 13194139535310 false] [17592186172238 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}8xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}y\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(2)^{2}y-4(-1)^{2}y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12y\\,dy \\\\  & =6{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535310 true] [17592186172241 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535310 true] [17592186172241 1100 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1, 0), (2, 0), (2, 2)$, and $(-1, 2)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n\n$F(x, y) = x^{2}yi + 5xyj$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535310 false] [17592186172241 1100 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+5xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535310 true] [17592186172241 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/265d27ad87a7f114bd2a32a5f48cbfedb13192abf48f9b759c4ed635e71ff144.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=5xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+5x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535310 true] [17592186343091 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343092 13194139535310 true] [17592186343091 1089 "q-127206" 13194139535310 true] [17592186343091 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535310 true] [17592186343091 1100 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨y+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and  $(0,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139535310 true] [17592186343091 1113 #uuid "b027c7d9-c33a-5bd1-a380-50294001a9fa" 13194139535310 true] [17592186343091 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/98d7da0c7cd1e1f1006c1db74ae1a1e1f198d9066df6585745ee36548f0b8d96.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+(-2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2xy.$" 13194139535310 true] [17592186343092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343093 13194139535310 true] [17592186343092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343094 13194139535310 true] [17592186343092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343095 13194139535310 true] [17592186343092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343096 13194139535310 true] [17592186343092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343097 13194139535310 true] [17592186343092 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535310 true] [17592186343092 1041 "selection" 13194139535310 true] [17592186343092 1104 #