 form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/23066b23aebf332921c22a0439a414ade8dffd2a270641d186da0ed0e9488821.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=12xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x{y}^{2}+12x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535310 true] [17592186343099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343100 13194139535310 true] [17592186343099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343101 13194139535310 true] [17592186343099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343102 13194139535310 true] [17592186343099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343103 13194139535310 true] [17592186343099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343104 13194139535310 true] [17592186343099 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535310 true] [17592186343099 1041 "selection" 13194139535310 true] [17592186343099 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343102 13194139535310 true] [17592186343099 1110 #uuid "967463aa-9fb3-5356-9566-5d49cd143f5e" 13194139535310 true] [17592186343100 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535310 true] [17592186343100 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535310 true] [17592186343100 1111 #uuid "7aaab870-fee8-5778-9e62-63cbd726d6ec" 13194139535310 true] [17592186343101 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}